\title{Draw-Regime Invariance of Weighted Covariance Dials\\[4pt]
\large A pairwise-functional analysis of sampling robustness, with an exact homotopy law,
an $\ell^1$ stability bound, and a concordance-budget triage rule}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{dial} is a scalar summary, computed from a weighted sample of a finite population,
intended to detect an association between two attributes of the population's elements. A
recurring concern in applied work is \emph{dilution}: the fear that an unbalanced sampling
scheme systematically attenuates a real association until it becomes undetectable, so that a
negative reading reflects the sampling scheme rather than the population.

We give a complete structural analysis of this concern for the canonical dial, the weighted
covariance. The organising principle is the weighted Hoeffding--Chebyshev \emph{pair identity}
\[
2\Cov_p(x,y) \;=\; \sum_{i}\sum_{j} p_i p_j\,(x_i-x_j)(y_i-y_j),
\]
which factors the dial into a \emph{population part} --- the concordance matrix
$D_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)(y_i-y_j)$, entirely free of the sampling weights --- and a \emph{regime
part} consisting of nonnegative pair masses $p_ip_j$. Because a draw regime supplies only
nonnegative weights, it can attenuate but never invert the population's verdict.

From this we derive: nonnegativity of the dial in \emph{every} draw regime for comonotone
populations, with strict positivity as soon as one strictly concordant pair is charged;
stability of the guarantee under arbitrary monotone re-encodings of either coordinate, so
that rank (Spearman-type) dials inherit it; a Lipschitz bound
$|\Cov_p - \Cov_q| \le M_xM_y\|p-q\|_1$; an exact quadratic law along the linear homotopy
between two regimes, with a universal $\tfrac12\min$ floor; a concordance/discordance budget
$\varepsilon^2 C - M^2\Delta \le 2\Cov_p$ yielding an explicit triage rule
$\kappa^2 < C/\Delta$ in terms of the regime's conditioning number $\kappa = M/\varepsilon$;
and a variance-share layer establishing weighted Cauchy--Schwarz, the identity
$\min\MSE = \Var_p(y)(1-R^2_p)$, an exact augmentation-gain formula
$\langle r,z\rangle_p^2/\|z\|_p^2$, and draw-invariant dominance of an exact affine driver.

We also delimit the result. The variance share $R^2$ is \emph{not} regime-invariant: a worked
four-key population exhibits a footprint predictor whose advantage over a count predictor
falls from $0.2117$ under a balanced regime to $0.1337$ under a regime at $\ell^1$ distance
$0.9$, while the ordering is preserved. What survives regime change is the sign, the ordering
under an exact driver, and $\ell^1$-controlled deviations --- not the numerical variance share.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} weighted covariance, Hoeffding identity, comonotonicity,
concordance, total variation, importance sampling, Spearman correlation, variance share,
Schur convexity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The dilution hypothesis}

Consider a finite population of items --- we call them \emph{keys} --- each carrying two real
attributes: a \emph{footprint} $x_i$ and a \emph{yield rate} $y_i$. A practitioner wishes to
know whether footprint predicts yield, and constructs a \emph{dial}: a single scalar,
computed from a weighted sample, positive when large footprints accompany large yields.

The dial must be computed under whatever sampling scheme is available, and in practice that
scheme is rarely uniform. This raises the \emph{dilution hypothesis}: under a genuinely
unbalanced draw, the measured association is systematically attenuated relative to a balanced
draw, potentially to the point of being undetectable. If this held, a dial would be unusable
outside carefully designed experiments. Our results refute it in a precise, structural sense,
while identifying the one component of the analysis that genuinely \emph{is} regime-dependent.

\subsection{Contributions}

We work with an arbitrary finite index set and impose no probabilistic model: no
independence, no distributional assumptions, no asymptotics. Everything is an exact algebraic
identity or a finite inequality.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Structural} (\S\ref{sec:pair}). The pair identity, and the consequent
representation of the dial as a nonnegatively-weighted sum over pairs of a regime-free
population quantity.
\item \textbf{Qualitative invariance} (\S\ref{sec:qual}). Sign invariance across all draw
regimes for comonotone populations; strict positivity under full support; stability under
monotone re-encoding, hence for rank dials.
\item \textbf{Quantitative stability} (\S\ref{sec:stab}). An $\ell^1$/total-variation
Lipschitz bound with explicit constants.
\item \textbf{Homotopy law} (\S\ref{sec:homotopy}). The exact quadratic in the mixing
parameter, nonnegativity of the cross term, and the $\tfrac12\min$ floor.
\item \textbf{Budget and triage} (\S\ref{sec:budget}). Concordance and discordance masses as
population invariants, the budget inequality, and the conditioning-number threshold.
\item \textbf{Variance-share layer} (\S\ref{sec:r2}). Weighted least squares,
Cauchy--Schwarz, $R^2$, augmentation gain, and draw-invariant dominance.
\item \textbf{A worked population and the boundary of the claim} (\S\ref{sec:example}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to classical material}

The unweighted case of the pair identity is classical, appearing in analyses of Chebyshev's
sum inequality and in Hoeffding's covariance representation. Comonotonicity is the discrete
shadow of the coupling notion used in dependence theory. What is new here is the \emph{use} of
these to answer a sampling-robustness question: treating balanced and unbalanced sampling as
two points of the same simplex, and reading off invariance, stability, homotopy and triage as
consequences of a single factorisation.

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $\iota$ is a finite index set with $n=|\iota|$ elements, and $x,y:\iota\to\R$ are
the footprint and yield attributes.

\begin{definition}[Draw regime]
A \emph{draw regime} on $\iota$ is a function $p:\iota\to\R$ with $p_i\ge0$ for all $i$ and
$\sum_i p_i = 1$. We write $\mathcal P(\iota)$ for the set of draw regimes.
\end{definition}

The \emph{uniform} (\emph{balanced}) regime is $p_i=1/n$; an \emph{unbalanced} regime is any
other; a regime has \emph{full support} if $p_i>0$ for all $i$. No structural distinction is
drawn between balanced and unbalanced regimes: they are points of the same convex set, and all
statements below quantify over the whole simplex.

\begin{definition}[Weighted moments]
For $p\in\mathcal P(\iota)$,
\[
\mu_p(x)=\sum_i p_ix_i,\qquad
\Cov_p(x,y)=\sum_i p_i\bigl(x_i-\mu_p(x)\bigr)\bigl(y_i-\mu_p(y)\bigr),\qquad
\Var_p(x)=\Cov_p(x,x).
\]
We call $\Cov_p(x,y)$ \emph{the dial}.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Symmetry]
$\Cov_p(x,y)=\Cov_p(y,x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Termwise commutativity of multiplication.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Raw-moment form]\label{lem:raw}
If $\sum_i p_i = 1$ then
$\Cov_p(x,y)=\sum_i p_ix_iy_i-\bigl(\sum_i p_ix_i\bigr)\bigl(\sum_i p_iy_i\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expand each summand as
$p_ix_iy_i-\mu_p(y)p_ix_i-\mu_p(x)p_iy_i+\mu_p(x)\mu_p(y)p_i$ and sum, using
$\sum_i p_i=1$: the last term becomes $\mu_p(x)\mu_p(y)$ and the two middle terms together
$-2\mu_p(x)\mu_p(y)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The pair identity}\label{sec:pair}

\begin{definition}[Concordance matrix]
For a population $(x,y)$ set $D_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)(y_i-y_j)$. The matrix $D$ is symmetric and
hollow ($D_{ii}=0$), and it is \emph{regime-free}: it depends on the population alone.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Weighted Hoeffding--Chebyshev pair identity]\label{thm:pair}
For every draw regime $p$,
\[
2\Cov_p(x,y)\;=\;\sum_i\sum_j p_i\,p_j\,D_{ij}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $D_{ij}=x_iy_i-x_iy_j-y_ix_j+x_jy_j$ and distribute against $p_ip_j$:
\[
\sum_{i,j}p_ip_jD_{ij}
=\sum_{i,j}\bigl(p_ix_iy_i\bigr)p_j-\sum_{i,j}(p_ix_i)(p_jy_j)
-\sum_{i,j}(p_iy_i)(p_jx_j)+\sum_{i,j}p_i\bigl(p_jx_jy_j\bigr).
\]
Each block is a product of single sums. Using $\sum_j p_j=1$, the first and fourth each equal
$\sum_i p_ix_iy_i$, while the second and third each equal $\mu_p(x)\mu_p(y)$. The right side is
therefore $2\bigl(\sum_i p_ix_iy_i-\mu_p(x)\mu_p(y)\bigr)$, which is $2\Cov_p(x,y)$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:raw}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why this is the whole story]\label{rem:whole}
Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} exhibits the dial as $\Cov_p(x,y)=\tfrac12 p^{\Tr}Dp$, a quadratic form
in the regime with a fixed, population-determined hollow symmetric matrix. Two consequences
drive everything below.
\begin{itemize}
\item The regime enters only through the \emph{nonnegative} pair masses $p_ip_j$. It reweights
the population's pairwise verdicts but cannot alter their signs.
\item The map $p\mapsto\Cov_p(x,y)$ is a polynomial of degree exactly $2$ on the simplex, so
all regime dependence is governed by the behaviour of $D$ on $\mathcal P(\iota)$: a question
of linear algebra, not of sampling theory.
\end{itemize}
\end{remark}

\section{Qualitative invariance}\label{sec:qual}

\begin{definition}[Comonotone population]
$(x,y)$ is \emph{comonotone} if $D_{ij}\ge0$ for all $i,j$: no pair of keys is discordant, i.e.
a larger footprint is never paired with a strictly smaller yield.
\end{definition}

Comonotonicity is purely ordinal: sorting by footprint also sorts by yield, ties permitted. It
requires no linearity, smoothness, or distributional structure.

\begin{theorem}[No dilution, qualitative form]\label{thm:nonneg}
If $(x,y)$ is comonotone then $\Cov_p(x,y)\ge0$ for \emph{every} draw regime $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every term $p_ip_jD_{ij}$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} is a product of nonnegative reals; a sum of
nonnegative reals is nonnegative; divide by $2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No dilution, strict form]\label{thm:pos}
Let $(x,y)$ be comonotone and $p$ a draw regime. If there are keys $a,b$ with $p_a>0$,
$p_b>0$ and $D_{ab}>0$, then $\Cov_p(x,y)>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
In the nonnegative double sum of Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} the single term $p_ap_bD_{ab}$ is
strictly positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Draw-regime invariance of the dial's sign]\label{cor:twoRegime}
Let $(x,y)$ be comonotone with at least one strictly concordant pair $D_{ab}>0$. Then for
\emph{any} two full-support draw regimes $p$ and $q$ --- for instance a balanced one and a
genuinely unbalanced one --- both $\Cov_p(x,y)>0$ and $\Cov_q(x,y)>0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pos} to each regime, full support supplying $p_a,p_b>0$.
\end{proof}

This is the formal content of the dilution refutation. To make a comonotone population's dial
vanish, a regime must remove \emph{all} strictly concordant pairs from its support; merely
underweighting them cannot do it. Attenuation of magnitude is possible; loss of signal is not.

\subsection{Monotone re-encoding and rank dials}

\begin{lemma}[Comonotonicity is ordinal]\label{lem:ordinal}
Let $(x,y)$ be comonotone and $g,h:\R\to\R$ nondecreasing. Then $(g\circ x,h\circ y)$ is
comonotone.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix $i,j$ and trichotomise on $x_i$ versus $x_j$. If $x_i<x_j$ then $y_i\le y_j$, since
otherwise $(x_i-x_j)(y_i-y_j)<0$ contradicts comonotonicity; monotonicity gives
$g(x_i)\le g(x_j)$ and $h(y_i)\le h(y_j)$, so the product of the two nonpositive differences is
$\ge0$. If $x_i=x_j$ the first factor vanishes. If $x_i>x_j$ the argument is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rank / Spearman-type invariance]\label{thm:rank}
Let $(x,y)$ be comonotone and $g,h$ nondecreasing. Then $\Cov_p(g\circ x,h\circ y)\ge0$ for
every draw regime $p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:ordinal} followed by Theorem~\ref{thm:nonneg}.
\end{proof}

Replacing an attribute by its rank is a nondecreasing re-encoding, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:rank} covers rank-based dials --- exactly what Spearman-type statistics
measure. The guarantee is robust to sampling scheme \emph{and} measurement scale.

\section{Quantitative stability in $\ell^1$}\label{sec:stab}

\begin{definition}
For regimes $p,q$ put $\|p-q\|_1=\sum_i|p_i-q_i|$, twice the total-variation distance.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Pair-mass estimate]\label{lem:pairmass}
For draw regimes $p,q$, \ $\sum_i\sum_j|p_ip_j-q_iq_j|\le2\|p-q\|_1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $p_ip_j-q_iq_j=p_i(p_j-q_j)+(p_i-q_i)q_j$ and apply the triangle inequality termwise:
\[
\sum_{i,j}|p_ip_j-q_iq_j|\le\Bigl(\sum_i p_i\Bigr)\|p-q\|_1+\|p-q\|_1\Bigl(\sum_j q_j\Bigr)
=2\|p-q\|_1 .\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Regime stability of the dial]\label{thm:stab}
Suppose $|x_i-x_j|\le M_x$ and $|y_i-y_j|\le M_y$ for all $i,j$. Then for any two draw regimes
$p,q$,
\[
\bigl|\Cov_p(x,y)-\Cov_q(x,y)\bigr|\;\le\;M_xM_y\,\|p-q\|_1 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\iota$ is empty both sides vanish; otherwise the range hypotheses force $M_x,M_y\ge0$.
Subtracting the two instances of Theorem~\ref{thm:pair},
$2\bigl(\Cov_p-\Cov_q\bigr)=\sum_{i,j}(p_ip_j-q_iq_j)D_{ij}$. Take absolute values, bound
$|D_{ij}|=|x_i-x_j|\,|y_i-y_j|\le M_xM_y$ inside the triangle inequality, and apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:pairmass} to obtain $2|\Cov_p-\Cov_q|\le M_xM_y\cdot 2\|p-q\|_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[``Identical within noise'', quantified]
Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} converts an informal experimental phrase into a theorem. Two regimes at
small $\ell^1$ distance \emph{must} report nearly equal dials, with Lipschitz constant
$M_xM_y$; conversely no adversarial reweighting can produce an unbounded change without moving
far in $\ell^1$.
\end{remark}

\section{The regime homotopy: an exact quadratic law}\label{sec:homotopy}

\begin{definition}[Regime mixture]
For $p,q:\iota\to\R$ and $t\in\R$, set $p^t_i=(1-t)p_i+tq_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:mix}
If $\sum_i p_i=\sum_i q_i=1$ then $\sum_i p^t_i=1$ for all $t$; and if $p,q\ge0$ and
$t\in[0,1]$ then $p^t\ge0$. Hence for $t\in[0,1]$ the mixture of two draw regimes is a draw
regime.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Linearity of the sum; convex combination of nonnegatives.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Cross term]
$K(p,q)=\tfrac12\sum_i\sum_j p_iq_jD_{ij}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cross}
$K(p,q)=K(q,p)$; and if $p,q\ge0$ and the population is comonotone then $K(p,q)\ge0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Symmetry: exchange the order of summation and use $D_{ji}=D_{ij}$. Nonnegativity: every
summand is a product of nonnegatives.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact quadratic law along a regime homotopy]\label{thm:quad}
For $p,q$ with $\sum_i p_i=\sum_i q_i=1$ and any $t\in\R$,
\[
\Cov_{p^t}(x,y)=(1-t)^2\Cov_p(x,y)+2t(1-t)K(p,q)+t^2\Cov_q(x,y).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} to $p^t$, legitimate by Lemma~\ref{lem:mix}, and expand
\[
p^t_ip^t_j=(1-t)^2p_ip_j+(1-t)t\,p_iq_j+t(1-t)\,q_ip_j+t^2q_iq_j .
\]
The first and last blocks are $2\Cov_p$ and $2\Cov_q$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:pair}; the two middle
blocks are equal (relabel and use symmetry of $D$), each contributing $2K(p,q)$. Divide by $2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Lower quadratic envelope]\label{cor:envelope}
If $p,q$ are draw regimes, the population is comonotone and $t\in[0,1]$, then
$\Cov_{p^t}(x,y)\ge(1-t)^2\Cov_p(x,y)+t^2\Cov_q(x,y)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Drop the middle term of Theorem~\ref{thm:quad}, nonnegative by Lemma~\ref{lem:cross} and
$2t(1-t)\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No dilution anywhere on the segment: the $\tfrac12\min$ floor]\label{thm:floor}
For a comonotone population and draw regimes $p,q$, every $t\in[0,1]$ satisfies
\[
\Cov_{p^t}(x,y)\;\ge\;\tfrac12\min\bigl(\Cov_p(x,y),\Cov_q(x,y)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $m=\min(\Cov_p,\Cov_q)\ge0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:nonneg}. By Corollary~\ref{cor:envelope},
$\Cov_{p^t}\ge((1-t)^2+t^2)m\ge\tfrac12 m$, the last step because
$(1-t)^2+t^2-\tfrac12=\tfrac12(2t-1)^2\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} rules out an \emph{interior collapse}: the dial cannot vanish, or even
lose more than a factor of two, at any intermediate mixing of two regimes that each read
positively. The case $t=\tfrac12$ with $K=0$ shows the constant $\tfrac12$ is of the right
order.
\end{remark}

\section{The concordance budget and a triage rule}\label{sec:budget}

\begin{definition}[Concordance and discordance mass]
\[
C(x,y)=\sum_i\sum_j\max(D_{ij},0),\qquad \Delta(x,y)=\sum_i\sum_j\max(-D_{ij},0).
\]
Both are nonnegative and depend on the population alone.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Signed budget identity]\label{prop:signed}
$C(x,y)-\Delta(x,y)=\sum_{i,j}D_{ij}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For each real $d$, $\max(d,0)-\max(-d,0)=d$; sum over pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Discordance characterises comonotonicity]\label{prop:disc}
$\Delta(x,y)=0$ if and only if $(x,y)$ is comonotone.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If all $D_{ij}\ge0$ then every $\max(-D_{ij},0)$ vanishes. Conversely $\Delta$ is a sum of
nonnegative terms, so $\Delta=0$ forces $D_{ij}\ge0$ for every pair.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Concordance budget]\label{thm:budget}
Let $\sum_i p_i=1$ with $0\le\varepsilon\le p_i\le M$ for every $i$. Then
\[
\varepsilon^2 C(x,y)-M^2\Delta(x,y)\;\le\;2\Cov_p(x,y).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Rewrite the left side pairwise as
$\sum_{i,j}\bigl(\varepsilon^2\max(D_{ij},0)-M^2\max(-D_{ij},0)\bigr)$; it suffices to prove
the inequality one pair at a time. The hypotheses give $\varepsilon^2\le p_ip_j\le M^2$, and
both $\max(D_{ij},0)$ and $\max(-D_{ij},0)$ are nonnegative, so
\[
\varepsilon^2\max(D_{ij},0)-M^2\max(-D_{ij},0)
\le p_ip_j\bigl(\max(D_{ij},0)-\max(-D_{ij},0)\bigr)=p_ip_jD_{ij}.
\]
Summing and invoking Theorem~\ref{thm:pair} completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Conditioning number]
If $0<\varepsilon\le p_i\le M$ for all $i$, the \emph{conditioning number} of $p$ relative to
these bounds is $\kappa=M/\varepsilon\ge1$; it equals $1$ exactly for the balanced regime.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Triage rule]\label{thm:triage}
Let $\sum_i p_i=1$ and $0<\varepsilon\le p_i\le M$. If $M^2\Delta(x,y)<\varepsilon^2C(x,y)$ ---
equivalently $\kappa^2<C(x,y)/\Delta(x,y)$ when $\Delta>0$ --- then $\Cov_p(x,y)>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:budget}: the left side of the budget is strictly positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Operational reading]
The threshold separates two independently available quantities. The ratio $C/\Delta$ is
computed once from the \emph{population}, before any sampling: a shape statistic measuring how
much pairwise evidence points the right way. The quantity $\kappa^2$ is a fact about the
\emph{sampling apparatus}. If $\kappa^2<C/\Delta$, positivity is guaranteed for every regime
respecting those bounds --- a certificate, not an estimate. Taking $\Delta=0$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:disc}) makes the ratio infinite and recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:pos}.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Sharpness at the balanced end]
For the uniform regime $\varepsilon=M=1/n$ the budget reads $C-\Delta\le2n^2\Cov_{\mathrm{unif}}$,
and Proposition~\ref{prop:signed} shows the left side equals $\sum_{i,j}D_{ij}
=2n^2\Cov_{\mathrm{unif}}$ exactly. The budget is therefore an equality when $\kappa=1$: the
entire loss in Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} is the price of imbalance.
\end{remark}

\section{The variance-share layer}\label{sec:r2}

\begin{definition}[Weighted MSE]
$\MSE_p(a,b)=\sum_i p_i(y_i-a-bx_i)^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:nonneg}
If $p\ge0$ then $\MSE_p(a,b)\ge0$ and $\Var_p(x)\ge0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Both are sums of $p_i$ times a square.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Centred sums vanish]\label{lem:centred}
If $\sum_i p_i=1$ then $\sum_i p_i(x_i-\mu_p(x))=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expand and use normalisation.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact MSE decomposition]\label{thm:mse}
For $\sum_i p_i=1$,
\[
\MSE_p(a,b)=\Var_p(y)-2b\Cov_p(x,y)+b^2\Var_p(x)+\bigl(\mu_p(y)-a-b\mu_p(x)\bigr)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $y_i-a-bx_i=(y_i-\mu_p(y))-b(x_i-\mu_p(x))+(\mu_p(y)-a-b\mu_p(x))$, square and sum
against $p_i$. The squared terms give $\Var_p(y)$, $b^2\Var_p(x)$ and the constant square; the
cross term pairing the two centred factors gives $-2b\Cov_p(x,y)$, and the two cross terms
involving the constant vanish by Lemma~\ref{lem:centred}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ordinary least squares optimum]\label{thm:ols}
If $\sum_i p_i=1$ and $\Var_p(x)>0$, then with
$b^\star=\Cov_p(x,y)/\Var_p(x)$ and $a^\star=\mu_p(y)-b^\star\mu_p(x)$,
\[
\MSE_p(a^\star,b^\star)=\Var_p(y)-\frac{\Cov_p(x,y)^2}{\Var_p(x)},
\]
and $\MSE_p(a,b)\ge\MSE_p(a^\star,b^\star)$ for all $a,b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substituting $a^\star,b^\star$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:mse} kills the constant square and leaves
the stated value. For optimality, Theorem~\ref{thm:mse} gives
\[
\MSE_p(a,b)-\MSE_p(a^\star,b^\star)
=\frac{\bigl(b\Var_p(x)-\Cov_p(x,y)\bigr)^2}{\Var_p(x)}+\bigl(\mu_p(y)-a-b\mu_p(x)\bigr)^2\ \ge\ 0 .
\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Weighted Cauchy--Schwarz]\label{thm:cs}
If $p\ge0$, $\sum_i p_i=1$ and $\Var_p(x)>0$, then
$\Cov_p(x,y)^2\le\Var_p(x)\Var_p(y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:nonneg} the optimal MSE of Theorem~\ref{thm:ols} is nonnegative, giving
$\Cov_p^2/\Var_p(x)\le\Var_p(y)$; multiply by $\Var_p(x)>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Variance share]
$R^2_p(x,y)=\dfrac{\Cov_p(x,y)^2}{\Var_p(x)\Var_p(y)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:r2range}
For $p\ge0$, $R^2_p\ge0$; and if in addition $\sum_i p_i=1$, $\Var_p(x)>0$ and $\Var_p(y)>0$,
then $R^2_p\le1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity is clear; the upper bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} divided by
$\Var_p(x)\Var_p(y)>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$R^2$ is the explained fraction]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:r2range},
$\MSE_p(a^\star,b^\star)=\Var_p(y)\bigl(1-R^2_p(x,y)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the definition of $R^2_p$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:ols}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Augmentation}

\begin{theorem}[Augmentation gain identity]\label{thm:aug}
Let $r,z:\iota\to\R$ and let $p$ satisfy $\sum_i p_iz_i^2>0$. With
$c^\star=\bigl(\sum_j p_jr_jz_j\bigr)\big/\bigl(\sum_j p_jz_j^2\bigr)$,
\[
\sum_i p_i(r_i-c^\star z_i)^2=\sum_i p_ir_i^2-\frac{\bigl(\sum_i p_ir_iz_i\bigr)^2}{\sum_i p_iz_i^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $p_i(r_i-cz_i)^2=p_ir_i^2-2c\,p_ir_iz_i+c^2p_iz_i^2$, sum, and substitute $c=c^\star$;
the cross term contributes $-2\langle r,z\rangle_p^2/\|z\|_p^2$ and the quadratic term
$+\langle r,z\rangle_p^2/\|z\|_p^2$.
\end{proof}

Here $\langle r,z\rangle_p=\sum_i p_ir_iz_i$ and $\|z\|_p^2=\sum_i p_iz_i^2$ are the inner
product and norm induced by the regime; Theorem~\ref{thm:aug} is the Pythagorean identity for
orthogonal projection in that geometry.

\begin{corollary}[Strict augmentation gain]\label{cor:aug}
If $\|z\|_p^2>0$ and $\langle r,z\rangle_p\ne0$ then
$\sum_i p_i(r_i-c^\star z_i)^2<\sum_i p_ir_i^2$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The subtracted quantity in Theorem~\ref{thm:aug} is strictly positive.
\end{proof}

Non-orthogonality of residual and new regressor is a regime-computable condition, which makes
Corollary~\ref{cor:aug} the operational justification for augmented-$R^2$ comparison.

\subsection{Exact drivers and draw-invariant dominance}

\begin{lemma}[Affine transport]\label{lem:affine}
If $\sum_i p_i=1$ and $\tilde y_i=a+bx_i$, then $\mu_p(\tilde y)=a+b\mu_p(x)$,
$\Cov_p(x,\tilde y)=b\Var_p(x)$ and $\Var_p(\tilde y)=b^2\Var_p(x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Direct computation, substituting $\mu_p(\tilde y)$ and simplifying termwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[An exact affine driver reads full variance share in every regime]\label{thm:affine1}
If $\sum_i p_i=1$, $\Var_p(x)>0$ and $\tilde y_i=a+bx_i$ with $b\ne0$, then
$R^2_p(x,\tilde y)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:affine},
$R^2_p=(b\Var_p(x))^2/\bigl(\Var_p(x)\cdot b^2\Var_p(x)\bigr)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Draw-invariant dominance]\label{thm:dominance}
Suppose $\tilde y_i=a+bx_i$ with $b\ne0$, $p\ge0$, $\sum_i p_i=1$, $\Var_p(x)>0$ and
$\Var_p(\tilde y)>0$. Then for any rival predictor $z$ with $\Var_p(z)>0$,
\[
R^2_p(z,\tilde y)\;\le\;R^2_p(x,\tilde y)=1 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:affine1} for the equality, Theorem~\ref{thm:r2range} for the inequality.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:dominance} is the structural ceiling behind the observation that a
footprint-weighted dial outperforms a plain count in both balanced and unbalanced regimes:
when footprint is the actual mechanism, no reweighting can promote a rival above it.

\section{A worked population, and the boundary of the claim}\label{sec:example}

Take four keys with
\[
x=(1,2,4,8)\ \text{(footprint)},\qquad z=(1,1,2,2)\ \text{(plain count)},\qquad
y=(1,2,5,9)\ \text{(yield rate)},
\]
and the two draw regimes
$p=(\tfrac14,\tfrac14,\tfrac14,\tfrac14)$ (balanced) and
$q=(\tfrac7{10},\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{10})$ (genuinely unbalanced).

\paragraph{Separation.} $\|p-q\|_1=\tfrac9{10}$: the regimes lie at total-variation distance
$0.45$.

\paragraph{Comonotonicity.} Both $x$ and $y$ increase strictly along the key order, so
$D_{ij}\ge0$ for all pairs; indeed $C(x,y)=266$ and $\Delta(x,y)=0$. By
Proposition~\ref{prop:disc} the population is comonotone, and by
Corollary~\ref{cor:twoRegime} both regimes must report a strictly positive dial. They do:
$\Cov_p(x,y)=8.3125$ and $\Cov_q(x,y)=5.47$.

\paragraph{Stability check.} The ranges are $M_x=7$, $M_y=8$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} permits
a drift of at most $7\cdot8\cdot0.9=50.4$. The actual drift is $2.8425$ --- well inside, as
expected for a bound that must also cover adversarial populations.

\paragraph{Homotopy check.} The cross term is $K(p,q)=8.5$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} predicts
$\Cov_{p^t}(x,y)=(1-t)^2(8.3125)+2t(1-t)(8.5)+t^2(5.47)$. At $t=\tfrac12$ this gives
$7.695625$, matching direct computation to machine precision. The $\tfrac12\min$ floor of
Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} is $2.735$; the true minimum along the segment is $5.47$, attained at
the endpoint.

\paragraph{Variance shares.}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
quantity & balanced $p$ & unbalanced $q$\\
\midrule
$R^2$ of footprint dial $x$ & $0.992370$ & $0.995277$\\
$R^2$ of plain count $z$    & $0.780645$ & $0.861544$\\
advantage $R^2(x)-R^2(z)$   & $+0.211725$ & $+0.133732$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Reading the table.} Two things happen at once, and separating them is the point.
The \emph{ordering} is stable: the footprint dial beats the plain count in both regimes, by a
wide margin in each, consistent with Theorem~\ref{thm:dominance} (here $y$ is not exactly
affine in $x$, but close, and the footprint's $R^2$ sits just below the ceiling of $1$). The
\emph{margin} is not stable: it drops from $0.2117$ to $0.1337$. The variance share is a ratio
of regime-dependent quantities and nothing in this framework makes it invariant.

This is the honest boundary of the phrase ``identical within noise''. What is invariant is the
sign of the dial (Theorems~\ref{thm:nonneg} and \ref{thm:pos}, Corollary~\ref{cor:twoRegime}),
the ordering under an exact driver (Theorem~\ref{thm:dominance}), and $\ell^1$-controlled
deviation of the covariance (Theorem~\ref{thm:stab}). What is not invariant is the numerical
variance share.

\section{Algorithms}

Let $n=|\iota|$.

\paragraph{Pairwise dial evaluation.} Compute $\Cov_p(x,y)$ either by the moment form
($\Theta(n)$ time) or by the pair form ($\Theta(n^2)$). The pair form is slower but exposes the
per-pair contributions $p_ip_jD_{ij}$, which identify which pairs of keys carry the signal and
which fight it.

\paragraph{Concordance-budget triage.} Given $x,y$ and imbalance bounds $\varepsilon\le M$,
accumulate $C$ and $\Delta$ in $\Theta(n^2)$ time and $\Theta(1)$ space and test
$M^2\Delta<\varepsilon^2C$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:triage} a positive test is a proof valid for
\emph{all} admissible regimes simultaneously --- no sampling required, no regime specified.

\paragraph{Homotopy audit.} Compute $\Cov_p$, $\Cov_q$ and $K(p,q)$ once ($\Theta(n^2)$), then
evaluate the quadratic of Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} at each grid point in $\Theta(1)$ apiece:
total $\Theta(n^2+m)$ for an $m$-point grid, versus $\Theta(mn)$ for naive re-evaluation. Since
the quadratic is exact, the minimum over $[0,1]$ is available in closed form: for an upward
parabola the vertex sits at $t^\star=(\Cov_p-K)/(\Cov_p-2K+\Cov_q)$, clipped to $[0,1]$.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Sampling design.} The triage rule inverts into a design constraint: given a
population's $C/\Delta$, the largest tolerable conditioning number is
$\kappa_{\max}=\sqrt{C/\Delta}$, and any scheme whose per-key inclusion masses stay within a
factor $\kappa_{\max}$ of each other is guaranteed to see the signal. The criterion is
checkable before data collection.

\paragraph{Importance sampling and reweighting.} Off-policy estimation reweights a sample to a
target distribution; Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} bounds how much the covariance estimate can move,
purely in terms of the $\ell^1$ shift, with no variance assumptions and no effective-sample-size
heuristic.

\paragraph{Robustness auditing.} Given an observed regime $q$ and a hypothetical reference $p$,
the homotopy audit produces the whole family of intermediate readings exactly, exhibiting the
worst case in closed form --- replacing ad hoc re-runs under a handful of reweightings.

\paragraph{Rank-based analyses.} Theorem~\ref{thm:rank} says the guarantee survives passage to
ranks, so one may work with rank dials where absolute scales are untrustworthy (subjective
ratings, censored measurements, heavy tails) at no loss of invariance.

\paragraph{Diagnostics for non-monotone structure.} A large $\Delta$ relative to $C$ signals a
genuinely non-monotone population, and the offending pairs are directly identifiable from the
pair-form evaluation --- turning a failed triage into a localisation of the exception.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the pair identity buys}

Every result above is obtained by bounding the \emph{same} nonnegative pair weights $p_ip_j$ in
a different way:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
bound applied to $p_ip_j$ & resulting theorem\\
\midrule
$p_ip_j\ge0$ & sign invariance (Thm.~\ref{thm:nonneg})\\
$p_ap_b>0$ for one pair & strict positivity (Thm.~\ref{thm:pos})\\
$\varepsilon^2\le p_ip_j\le M^2$ & concordance budget (Thm.~\ref{thm:budget})\\
$\sum_{i,j}|p_ip_j-q_iq_j|\le2\|p-q\|_1$ & $\ell^1$ stability (Thm.~\ref{thm:stab})\\
bilinear expansion of $p^t_ip^t_j$ & exact quadratic law (Thm.~\ref{thm:quad})\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Draw-regime invariance is therefore \emph{structural} rather than incidental: it uses no
property of the sampling scheme beyond nonnegativity and normalisation.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Finite populations only.} Everything is a finite sum; extension to general
probability spaces should be routine via the Hoeffding representation, but is not carried out
here.
\item \textbf{Comonotonicity is a real hypothesis.} It is ordinal and weak, but not vacuous:
populations with genuine exceptions require the budget of \S\ref{sec:budget}, and the triage
rule may be inconclusive.
\item \textbf{$R^2$ is not invariant}, as \S\ref{sec:example} documents. Any claim of regime
invariance for a variance share needs an extra hypothesis, such as the exact-driver condition
of Theorem~\ref{thm:dominance}.
\item \textbf{The stability constant is conservative.} $M_xM_y$ is a worst-case range product
and will overstate achievable drift for populations concentrated away from their extremes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Interpreting the empirical picture}

Augmented variance shares that agree closely between a uniform and a balanced regime are what
Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} forces when the regimes are $\ell^1$-close. Rank-correlation readings
that stay comfortably positive in both regimes are Theorem~\ref{thm:rank}. A
footprint-weighted dial beating a plain count in \emph{both} regimes is the observable shadow
of Theorem~\ref{thm:dominance}. The residual regime dependence --- margin shrinking while
ordering holds --- is precisely the boundary drawn in \S\ref{sec:example}, not an anomaly.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. Discordance spectrum of the pair form.} The dial is the quadratic form
$p\mapsto\tfrac12p^{\Tr}Dp$ with $D$ hollow symmetric; all regime dependence is carried by the
behaviour of $D$ on the simplex, so dilution is an eigenvalue question rather than a sampling
question. \emph{Conjecture:} $D$ has exactly one positive eigenvalue when the population is
comonotone with distinct footprints, and the ratio of the largest negative to the largest
positive eigenvalue is exactly the flip threshold $C/\Delta$ up to a factor $n$.

\paragraph{2. Majorization order on draw regimes.} ``Unbalancedness'' is arguably better
measured by majorization than by $\ell^1$. \emph{Conjecture:} for comonotone populations with
$D$ entrywise monotone in the key order, $p\prec q$ implies $\Cov_q\le\Cov_p$, with equality
iff $p$ and $q$ agree on the support of the concordant pairs. The pair identity makes the dial
an explicit symmetric quadratic, exactly the setting where Schur-convexity criteria apply.

\paragraph{3. Augmentation saturation.} Corollary~\ref{cor:aug} shows each new regressor
strictly improves the fit by $\langle r,z\rangle_p^2/\|z\|_p^2$. \emph{Conjecture:} the total
achievable gain over an arbitrary family of footprint-derived regressors is bounded by the
population's discordance mass, so that the augmented-$R^2$ plateau is a population invariant
rather than an artefact of the regressor list; specifically, the supremum over finite regressor
families of the augmented $R^2$ equals $1-\Delta/(C+\Delta)$ for rank-encoded dials.

\section{Conclusion}

The dilution hypothesis conflates two distinct phenomena. An unbalanced draw does see a
\emph{smaller} covariance --- concentration reduces spread, hence the absolute magnitude of any
second moment. It does not see a \emph{weaker relationship}. The direction of association is
determined by the population's pairwise concordance structure, and a draw regime contributes
only nonnegative pair weights to that structure. Volume, yes; tune, no.

The pair identity $2\Cov_p(x,y)=\sum_{i,j}p_ip_jD_{ij}$ is the whole mechanism. It separates
population from sample so cleanly that four distinct robustness questions --- sign, magnitude
drift, interpolation, and the effect of exceptions --- each reduce to a different elementary
bound on the same nonnegative weights. Any statistic admitting such a representation inherits
the same inventory of guarantees.

